  actress to trick your brain into  believing you are the person you know  and and if you trick your brain well  enough your body starts to respond as if  you are that person you know so  situations that are not dangerous  because you're an actor and it's an  we are read by your body as dangerous  and your heart will race your sweat  you'll you'll have a physiological  reaction that's not of your making you  know and and and that those experiences  in the body never leave you I think I  mean there is so much pressure in  playing a real person especially someone  who was as fiercely loved and missed as  Murray  I mean how Murray's friends are devoted  her family you know it's it's a painful  recent loss for them her family are  fighting at the moment they're bringing  a court case against the Syrian regime  for Marie's murder they really believe  and have a body of evidence to support  the fact that she was targeted by the  Assad regime when she was when she went  into homes I mean it was an enormous  pressure because because in this  instance and I don't think it's the same  with every real person you play  sometimes I think you can have a  legitimate choice as an actor to get a  feeling of the person across or tell the  truth of their story but not you know  embody them fully but in this III had a  documentary maker as my director who is  used solely to trading in the truth in  real people in observing real people and  secondly I found Marie such an  intoxicating woman when I listened to  her watched that I just thought I want  to put that on screen I want to be as  close as I possibly can to her I want to  be her I want to apologize for not being  her you know with a documentary maker as  my director so I just tried to do  everything I could to kind of eradicate  all trace of my own instincts and  behaviors and once I decided to do that  I then realized what a what a momentous  task it was you know to change a voice  change your posture change the way you  walk change change the little tells the  little insecurities that you know you  default to in an uncomfortable situation  learn to smoke and that's just the  external there's a whole world of  internal stuff too of course  one of my heroes I ended up playing  right after I played Marie Colvin and  that was Marie Curie so I had this very  extraordinary year where two very  important Marie's came into my hands at  the same time and that was you know they  definitely influence one another in my  mind I think because I was I was sort of  thinking about them both in tandem  sometimes and Marie Curie was just an  astonishing person again someone who  lived courageously  I mean Marie Curie probably I would say  lived pretty fearless Lee Marie Colvin I  don't think was fearless  you know that's often the cliche about  the war correspondent is that they're  fearless I think she was not Phyllis at  all I think she had tremendous fear but  the need to tell the story was so urgent  that she went past her fear apart from  that there's no one there's no one  currently calling me no but give me a  year  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]

